steps.body:
The Recursion lesson halved a sorted range with recursive calls. A `while` loop does the same with no call stack: keep the range `lo` to `hi`, look at the middle, and throw away the half that can't hold the target.

```java
static int search(int[] a, int target) {
    int lo = 0;
    int hi = a.length - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (a[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        } else if (a[mid] < target) {
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            hi = mid - 1;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
System.out.println(search(new int[]{2, 4, 7, 9}, 7)); // 2
```

`while (lo <= hi)` runs while the range holds at least one index, and every pass shrinks it, so the loop always ends. Write the middle as `lo + (hi - lo) / 2`: on a huge array `lo + hi` can pass `Integer.MAX_VALUE` and wrap to a negative index. For an array sorted from high to low, flip the two comparisons: go right when `a[mid]` is bigger than the target.

`Arrays.binarySearch(a, x)` runs this search for you on an ascending array. When `x` is missing, it returns a negative number, `-(insertion point) - 1`, where the insertion point is the index `x` would take to keep the array sorted. Turn it back with `-r - 1`:

```java
int[] a = {2, 4, 7, 9};
int r = Arrays.binarySearch(a, 5);
System.out.println(r + " " + (-r - 1)); // -3 2
```

