steps.body:
With duplicates, an exact match can land on any copy. A **lower bound** finds the first index whose value is at least the target, and an **upper bound** the first index whose value is greater than it. Both use a half-open range: `hi` starts at `a.length`, one past the end, and the loop runs `while (lo < hi)`:

```java
static int lowerBound(int[] a, int target) {
    int lo = 0;
    int hi = a.length;
    while (lo < hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (a[mid] < target) {
            lo = mid + 1;        // mid is too small: the answer is after it
        } else {
            hi = mid;            // mid might be the answer: keep it
        }
    }
    return lo;
}
System.out.println(lowerBound(new int[]{1, 2, 2, 2, 5}, 2)); // 1
```

The upper bound is the same method with `a[mid] <= target` in the `if`. Both return `a.length` when no value qualifies, so check that before you read `a[lo]`.

Together they answer the duplicate questions. The target appears `upper - lower` times. Its first index is `lower` when `a[lower]` equals it, and its last index is `upper - 1`. Never write `lo = mid` in this loop: `mid` rounds down, so a range of two would never shrink.

