steps.body:
Sometimes you don't search an array at all. You search for the smallest value that works, when every bigger value works too. Write a check for one guess, then binary search the range of possible answers with the lower-bound loop: when `mid` works, keep it as a candidate with `hi = mid`, otherwise go higher with `lo = mid + 1`.

Koko eats bananas at `speed` per hour, from one pile at a time, and must finish every pile within `h` hours:

```java
static boolean canFinish(int[] piles, int speed, int h) {
    long hours = 0;
    for (int p : piles) {
        hours += (p + speed - 1) / speed;   // p / speed, rounded up
    }
    return hours <= h;
}
System.out.println(canFinish(new int[]{3, 6, 7, 11}, 4, 8)); // true
```

`(p + speed - 1) / speed` rounds up with no `double`: a pile of 7 at speed 4 takes 2 hours. The total is a `long`, since many large piles can pass the `int` limit. Then search the speeds from 1 up to the biggest pile, which always works:

```java harness
static boolean canFinish(int[] piles, int speed, int h) { long hours = 0; for (int p : piles) hours += (p + speed - 1) / speed; return hours <= h; }
int[] piles = {3, 6, 7, 11};
int h = 8;
int biggest = 11;
{{code}}
System.out.println(lo);
```

```java
int lo = 1;
int hi = biggest;
while (lo < hi) {
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    if (canFinish(piles, mid, h)) {
        hi = mid;              // works: try slower
    } else {
        lo = mid + 1;          // too slow
    }
}
```

