    static boolean canShip(int[] weights, int cap, int days) {
        int used = 1;
        int load = 0;
        for (int w : weights) {
            if (load + w > cap) {
                used++;
                load = 0;
            }
            load += w;
        }
        return used <= days;
    }
}

steps.body:
A sorted array turned at some point, like `{4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2}`, is no longer sorted, but binary search still works on it. Cut any range in the middle and at least one half is still in order. Find that half by comparing its two ends, check whether the target lies inside it, and keep whichever half can hold the target:

```java
static int searchRotated(int[] a, int target) {
    int lo = 0;
    int hi = a.length - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (a[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        }
        if (a[lo] <= a[mid]) {                          // the left half is in order
            if (a[lo] <= target && target < a[mid]) {
                hi = mid - 1;
            } else {
                lo = mid + 1;
            }
        } else {                                        // the right half is in order
            if (a[mid] < target && target <= a[hi]) {
                lo = mid + 1;
            } else {
                hi = mid - 1;
            }
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
System.out.println(searchRotated(new int[]{4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2}, 0)); // 4
```

`a[lo] <= a[mid]` means the left half climbs with no break, so the target is in it exactly when `a[lo] <= target && target < a[mid]`. Otherwise the break is on the left, the right half is in order, and the same test uses `a[mid]` and `a[hi]`. The `<=` in `a[lo] <= a[mid]` matters when `lo` and `mid` are the same index. This assumes no value repeats.

